Load rendering throttle, part 2: hook the LayerTreeHost run-loop observer.

Device A/B on build 465 showed the throttle was INERT (warm-cache
franceinfo: DCL 4237ms off vs 4490ms on, tile-paint passes 394 vs 395):
on this port rendering updates are NOT driven by WebCore's
RenderingUpdateScheduler/DisplayLink fps machinery that part 1 clamps -
WebChromeClient::scheduleRenderingUpdate() routes to the DrawingArea and
LayerTreeHost schedules its run-loop observer directly, so the update
cadence never consulted Page::preferredRenderingUpdateInterval().

Apply the throttle where this port actually paces rendering updates:
LayerTreeHost::scheduleRenderingUpdate() defers the run-loop observer
with a one-shot timer until WEBKIT_LOAD_RENDERING_INTERVAL_MS has elapsed
since the last update, while Page::isRenderingThrottledForLoad() (the
ProgressTracker main-load window from part 1) is active. Dirty regions
keep accumulating; the batched update paints the union once. Part 1's
Page-side clamp stays (harmless, and correct for any future scheduler
use); this adds the missing enforcement point. Applies ON TOP of
webkit-load-rendering-throttle-env.patch.

